STARTUP PROGRESSION, np8 (each step removes one O(N^2) serial piece):
                              N=50000        N=100000
  pre-startup-decomp        : 43s (ROSE)     ~198s (ROSE with np)
  + FPOLY2 decomposed       : 31s (floor)    (not separately measured at 1e5)
  + FPOLY0 decomposed (now) : 12s            49s   (179->49 = 3.65x, ~83% parallel)
RAW in-code wall np8 speedup:
  N=50000 : 1.66x -> 2.04x (FPOLY2) -> 3.43x (FPOLY0)   [integ ceiling 3.62x]
  N=100000: 1.67x -------------------> 3.90x (FPOLY0)   [integ ceiling 4.10x]
At 1e5 the raw wall now essentially MATCHES the integration speedup (3.90 vs
4.10x) -- the "raw wall is startup-bound at large N" finding from the 2026-06-06
sweep (raw np8 only 1.67x) is RESOLVED. Startup parallel fraction RISES with N
(N=50000 ~78%, N=100000 ~83%) as the O(N^2) force builds grow vs the O(N) setup
remainder. Integration speedup unchanged (np8 4.10x, p 0.864) -- path untouched.

REMAINING ~12s startup floor at np8 = the still-serial parts of START outside the
two force builds: INPUT/DATA/SCALE (IC generation), GPUNB_OPEN/SEND + GPUIRR setup
(replicated by design -- every rank needs the full j-state), KSPINIT, and the
replicated XTRNLD/assembly post-loops. These are O(N) or setup-bound, not O(N^2),
so the dominant large-N startup cost is now addressed. Further trimming (rank-0
I/O, overlap) is lower-yield -- see the integration-serial-trim lever (Path 2).
